+# Blueprint: Trading Notes Analysis (due Oct 23, 5:00 p.m. ET)
+
+For the team's use only: content, structure and checks, not text to submit. The team writes every word.
+Sources: `competition/official/2026_27/2026_WGY_Trading_Notes_Analysis-FINAL.txt` (TN), SMApply deliverables page (D),
+client case (C). Status: DRAFT for team approval.
+
+## 1. What is actually required (official)
+- Select **3 Trading Notes from trades executed in WInS**; include each **exactly as it appears in WInS**; "Yes, we will
+  verify this" (TN p.2).
+- For each: a reflection of **100 words or fewer** explaining (TN p.2):
+  1. why the team made the decision;
+  2. how it aligned with the overall investment strategy;
+  3. how it supported Laura's goals, funding needs, or risk considerations.
+- Together the three should show "individual investments as part of a cohesive portfolio strategy"; quality of
+  reasoning matters, **not** whether the trade made money (TN p.1).
+- Not expected: reserve size, long-term projections, facility contribution, co-sponsor text (TN p.1).
+- Trades need not still be held at the end; a later sale can still be a good example (TN p.1).
+- Must be consistent with the strategy the IPS will state on Nov 6; all three deliverables are judged together (C p.4).
+- Evaluation criteria that apply most: Investment Strategy (consistency, diversification), Client Knowledge,
+  Articulation of Competition Experience (decision process, learning) (D).
+
+## 2. The three trades to aim for (each shows a different part of the strategy)
+| # | Trade type | Strategy role it proves | What makes it strong |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **Treasury hedge purchase**: IEF + TLT in the duration-matching mix (~65/35 of the Treasury part) | Protect the ten fixed payments first ("the promise") | Shows the case's core insight: a fixed $50k stream is matched by bonds whose value moves like the payments. Wharton's own example note is a Treasury ETF bought to prepare for the operating commitment (TN p.2), so this is squarely on target. |
+| 2 | **Growth purchase**: broad equity (e.g. VTI, plus ex-U.S. such as VXUS/VEA) | Only money the promise doesn't need takes market risk | Shows risk is taken deliberately and diversified, tied to the facility contribution and flexibility. |
+| 3 | **Discipline trade**: a rebalance back to target after a market move, OR a short-Treasury/T-bill position illustrating the 2031 floor idea | Rules over reactions (team pre-mortem) | Shows process and learning (Articulation criterion). A "we did NOT chase X" rebalance is very strong if it really happened. |
+Back-ups: a trade that tested and refined the plan (e.g. adjusting the IEF/TLT mix after checking official durations).
+
+## 3. What to put in the WInS note at the moment of trading (elements, in the team's words)
+The note is quoted verbatim later, so write it well at trade time. Each note should contain:
+- the action and instrument (buy/sell, ticker, rough portfolio share);
+- the role in Laura's plan (which "job": the promise, the growth sleeve, or discipline);
+- the reason now (the signal or rule that triggered it);
+- the main risk accepted (e.g. bond prices fall if rates rise, which does not matter for held-to-maturity matching).
+Keep it short and plain English. Check the WInS character limit first.
+
+## 4. Reflection checklist (per note, <=100 words)
+- [ ] Answers all three required questions (why / strategy fit / Laura's needs), in that order or clearly.
+- [ ] Names Laura's specific need (fixed $50k payments 2033-2042, facility contribution, credibility in 2031), not
+      generic "diversification".
+- [ ] Uses at most one number, and it is verified (e.g. "payments cost about $292k in today's Treasury prices").
+- [ ] Mentions a trade-off or what was learned (this is what separates strong teams).
+- [ ] No jargon without meaning (say "moves like the payments when rates change", not "DV01").
+- [ ] Consistent with the other two notes and the planned IPS (same strategy words, same percentages).
+- [ ] Count: <=100 words.
+
+## 5. Judge red flags to avoid
+- Hot themes that don't fit the mandate (the team's own pre-mortem: ARKK/MSOS-type "storyteller" trades).
+- Justifying by returns ("it went up 3%").
+- Any number that contradicts the IPS or Final Report.
+- Notes written after the fact (they must match WInS exactly).
+
+## 6. First-trade plan (pending this year's approved list, starting cash and sector rules)
+Target WInS mix (mirrors Laura's plan after the 2028 deposit):
+| Sleeve | Share of portfolio | Instruments (if approved) | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Treasury hedge | ~65% | ~42% IEF + ~23% TLT (duration ~9.9y, verified fact sheets 6.95y/15.31y) | Moves like Laura's ten payments |
+| Growth | ~35% | ~25% VTI + ~10% VXUS or VEA | Broad, cheap, global; JPM expects ~6.7-7.5% compound |
+| Cash | small | BIL/SHV if needed | Trading float only |
+Open: if WInS requires a minimum number of **sectors** and does not count broad ETFs toward it, the growth sleeve may need
+sector funds (e.g. XLK, XLV, XLF, XLE, XLI, XLP) instead of VTI; confirm how WInS counts sectors before trading.
+
+## 7. Timeline
+- Week of Sep 28: place trades 1 and 2 with good WInS notes.
+- Oct 1-16: at least one discipline/rebalance decision, logged in the team's decision log.
+- Oct 12-20: pick the best 3, write reflections; assistant reviews against Sections 1 and 4.
+- Oct 21: team approval; Oct 23: submit via SMApply (5:00 p.m. ET).
